Delete:
i)Leaf node.(having no children)
 If the target node is the leaf node, it can be directly removed
 from the tree since it has no child to maintain.
 
ii)One child.(left or right)
If the target node has only one child, we remove the node and 
connect its parent to its only child.

iii)Two child.(left and right both)
If the target node has two children, to maintain the BST property, we
need to find a replacement node for the target.

The replacement can be either:

 a) The inorder successor: the smallest value in the left subtree,
 which is the next greater value than the target node.
 
 b) The inorder predecessor: the larget value in the right subtree,
 which is the next smaller value than the target node.
 
 
        50
       /  \
      30  70
     / \  / \
    20 40 60 80
    
    target : 50
    
        60
       / \
      30  70
     / \   \
    20 40   80
    
    
    search : 40
    
 Traversing: In-order(Sorted order)   - left-root-right
    
    
20,30,40,60,70,80

Advanatages:
i)Fast searching
ii)Dynamic size
iii)Efficient insert/delete
iv)Sorted data retrival

Disadvantages:
i)Can be skiwed
ii)complex than array


Time complexity:

i)Insert: O(1), O(log n), O(n)
ii)Delete:O(1), O(log n), O(n)
iii)Search:O(1), O(log n), O(n)
iv)Traversal: O(n)


BST Validation:

   10
   /\ 
  5  15
      /
     6
 Inorder: 5 10 6 15  
 
 Step 1: Perform inorder traversal
 step 2: Store the previous visited nodes
 step 3: current node must be greater than previous node 
 step 4: If not, tree is invald
 
 
 Kth Smallest element in BST:
 
         5
        / \
       3   7
      / \ / \  
     2  4 6  8
     
     k=4 
  
  Inorder traversal:  2 -> 3 -> 4 ->5 ->6 ->7 ->8   
  
  kth smallest element : 5
  
  
  Convert a sorted array/list to BST:
  
  [-10,-3,0,5,9]
  
  
   0 - middle point of the array
   [-10,-3] [5,9]
   
    0
   / \
  -3  9
  /    /
  -10 5
  
  step 1: find middle index
  step 2: create root node
  step 3: recursively build left subtree from left half.
  step 4: recursively build roght subtree from right half
  
  
  Create a BST from a given sorted linkedlist.
  
  Method  1: convert linkedlist to array then apply previous solution.
  Method  2: Two pointers technique. 
  
  
  Heaps and Priority Queues
  
  
  
  Heap:A binary heap is a complete binary tree, meaning all levels
  are filled except possible the last, which is filled from left to
  right.
  
  Min-heap:The value of the root node is the smallest and this rule
  also applies to all subtrees.
  
  
  Max-heap: The value of the root node is the largest, and this property
  is true for all subtrees.
  
  
      2                             8
     / \                           / \
    3   4                         7   6
    /\  /\                       /\   /\
   5  6 7 8                      5  4 3 2 
   
   A binary heap is represented using an array.
   
   the root element will be at arr[0]
   indices of the other nodes for ith node i.e. arr[i]
   
   arr[(i-1)/2]  - returns the parent node
   arr[(2*i)+1]  - returns the left child node
   arr[(2*i)+2]  - returns the right child
   
   
   
